---
Checks: '
  bugprone-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  modernize-*,
  performance-*,
  readability-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -modernize-use-trailing-return-type,
  -readability-convert-member-functions-to-static,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-magic-numbers
'
# Why each of the five above is off. They were unexplained for as long as this
# file has existed, which is what makes them relitigable - none is a hard call,
# and a line each is cheaper than having the argument twice.
#
#   bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#     Fires on any two adjacent parameters of the same type. Nearly every
#     `(host, path)`, `(name, value)` and `(from, to)` in the engine is one, and
#     the check's own remedy - strong typedefs on every pair - is a rewrite of
#     the whole surface, not a fix for a defect anybody has hit here.
#   modernize-use-trailing-return-type
#     `auto f() -> T` is a style this codebase does not use. Turning it on
#     rewrites every declaration in the tree and settles nothing.
#   readability-convert-member-functions-to-static
#     Proposes making a member static whenever its body happens not to touch
#     `this`. On a public class that is an API change decided by an
#     implementation detail, and the next commit that reads a field reverses it.
#   readability-identifier-length
#     `i`, `n`, `it`, `os` in a three-line scope are idiomatic and clearer than
#     the names the check would demand.
#   readability-magic-numbers
#     The engine is full of protocol constants (status codes, ports, curl
#     option values) and test literals. Naming each at its single use site moves
#     the number away from what it means.

# For the pre-commit hook, which lints against whatever build tree you already
# have. The engine builds with a precompiled header, and on a GCC build that
# `.gch` is not one clang can read, so clang-tidy replaying the compile command
# says "precompiled header ... was ignored because it is not a clang PCH file"
# on every file. It then falls back to including the header as text and analyses
# exactly the same translation unit, so the diagnostic is noise about the build
# tree rather than a fact about the code - and a hook that prints it once per
# staged file is a hook people stop reading.
#
# It has to be a compiler flag: with -Werror in the compile command clang raises
# it as a hard error before any check filter is consulted, so a
# `-clang-diagnostic-ignored-gch` entry in the check list above does nothing.
#
# CI does not rely on this. It regenerates `compile_commands.json` with
# `-DCMAKE_DISABLE_PRECOMPILE_HEADERS=ON` before linting, which is the fix that
# holds for every compiler - macOS produces a *clang* PCH that Homebrew's
# clang 19 still rejects as "a newer PCH format", which no `-Wno-` flag covers.
# The hook does not do that, because reconfiguring someone's build tree from a
# git hook would throw away their next incremental build.
ExtraArgs: ['-Wno-ignored-gch']

# A finding is a failure, in one place. Both consumers of this config read their
# verdict from clang-tidy's exit status - the `scripts/pre-commit` hook and the
# `Lint changed engine sources` step in `.github/workflows/pr-tests.yml` - and
# with this empty, clang-tidy exits 0 having printed every diagnostic it found.
# That is the shape issue #885 was filed about: three layers of linting, none of
# which could stop anything. Set here rather than as a `--warnings-as-errors`
# flag at each call site so the two cannot disagree about what counts.
WarningsAsErrors: '*'
HeaderFilterRegex: '.*/(?:src|include|tests)/.*'
ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex: '.*/vendor/.*'
FormatStyle: file
...
